e\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(8{x}^{12})={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{12})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-99275/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-99275"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-301610/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "e445182a-9d22-50fa-884c-cc6da48707f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-301610/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "f613d2cb-38d1-5f1e-ac6e-57a420181f19" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-301610/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-301610/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-301610" :question/id #uuid "6c99095c-ef3c-5150-9fbc-24c606d52761" :question/math-academy-id "q-301610" :question/is-example false :question/problem "If $p(x)=-20{x}^{6}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}$, then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x))=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(p(x)\\cdot q(x)) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-20{x}^{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{5{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }((-20\\cdot \\frac{1}{5})\\frac{{x}^{6}}{{x}^{4}}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-4$ causes $y=-4{x}^{2}$ to be a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-4{x}^{2})=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-301610/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-301610"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection/c5e624c1da93ecfedccf3fa70b8e1fb64de1e5d703dcc02fa7b4793f81a70f81" :answer/id #uuid "1e41fd19-d0da-5a59-af5e-53883bf0853c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer/id #uuid "63eb7aca-0d25-54e9-9815-8e7de92cc624" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "988c9df7-7d31-5c0b-84c8-6101237506c7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" :answer/id #uuid "03120a11-37f0-519b-84a3-ebf01005aff8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection/7bc54ef2cebd9c4cf6fef3072c83d13d0dfd6652d02cb9919fbcbc5497b7a408" :answer/id #uuid "3f34a6f5-2d43-5a3e-880a-469aeb9abb1b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "6723670a-201b-5223-93b1-babf84c63637" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-35243/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" :answer-field/choices ["q-35243/selection/c5e624c1da93ecfedccf3fa70b8e1fb64de1e5d703dcc02fa7b4793f81a70f81" "q-35243/selection/64ee21896e4acdba2b7ccd5d21e7c3b0caad319fb011e671d6ce72f671b3bb6c" "q-35243/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" "q-35243/selection/62a6fd1cf465955cead36d661dcbed2f93206049c15d21d7eebcabb802d1aa54" "q-35243/selection/7bc54ef2cebd9c4cf6fef3072c83d13d0dfd6652d02cb9919fbcbc5497b7a408"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-35243" :question/id #uuid "ddcba296-0d72-577a-b13c-e8483070e583" :question/math-academy-id "q-35243" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930904/assets/67b77d2d3798e87593c7dddeea93603fff36d9153f53248cc2dab295f4879321.png)\n\nThe figure above shows the graph of $f(x).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)}.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the quotient rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot ({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{({lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x))}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }f(x)=3.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}}{f(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{3}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{2}$ is an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{2}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $\\frac{1}{3}$ does not change the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2})={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{2})=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-35243/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-35243"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-301603/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "e3b189bc-e353-56e7-b35e-490e5768f4f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-301603/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "15a47ab1-576b-547c-8657-c38701392840" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-301603/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-301603/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-301603" :question/id #uuid "5ffbc29c-ac41-5b4d-9058-2b01467fe05a" :question/math-academy-id "q-301603" :question/is-example false :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13930904/assets/2fd351e75519ab242ba99028214cbdd35c61cea6acdf69a39eb30395e2f7eaaa.png)\n\nIf the figure above shows the graph of $f(x),$ then\n\n${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5})=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Applying the product rule and the root rule, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{f(x)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }\\sqrt[5]{f(x)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the graph, we get that ${lim}_{x\\to \\infty }f(x)=-32.$ Therefore, the above becomes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6}{(f(x))}^{1/5}) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot \\sqrt[5]{-32} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }-2{x}^{6} \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-2$ causes $y=-2{x}^{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(-2{x}^{6})=-{lim}_{x\\to \\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-301603/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4974097a-95c9-5835-ba1d-46480efab66b"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-301603"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-336894/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "3b685083-a288-5948-8f2b-fdc648a7b75c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336894/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "76908e74-15e1-5188-b779-5628ee8ca367" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336894/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer-field/choices ["q-336894/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336894" :question/id #uuid "c5cab8eb-177c-5cf6-8c20-f7ff9d55c831" :question/math-academy-id "q-336894" :question/is-example false :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6\\cdot {x}^{7}) \\\\  & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{7}$ is a power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-6$ changes the sign of the limit. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{7}=\\infty . & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-6{x}^{7})=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336894/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336894"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-35192/selection/c5e624c1da93ecfedccf3fa70b8e1fb64de1e5d703dcc02fa7b4793f81a70f81" :answer/id #uuid "ab633047-54e9-510b-a7ea-63f8cec1a71e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\text{DNE}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186103745 :answer-field/choices ["q-35192/selection/c5e624c1da93ecfedccf3fa70b8e1fb64de1e5d703dcc02fa7b4793f81a70f81"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-35192"] :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {x}^{5}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{5}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the negative constant $-\\frac{1}{4}$ changes the sign of the limit. \nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5} & =-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{5}=\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(-\\frac{{x}^{5}}{4})=\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-336891/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "3431c9ad-3de8-5f6b-98b3-c0f145772b87" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-336891/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4f6ee83b-edad-50ad-a28f-54f54db808ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-336891/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer-field/choices ["q-336891/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-336891" :question/id #uuid "4f83014e-2628-5f35-ab54-6db1659f5989" :question/math-academy-id "q-336891" :question/is-example false :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13}) & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4\\cdot {x}^{13}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe graph $y={x}^{13}$ is power function with an odd positive power and therefore has the property that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\n$$\n\nMultiplication by the positive constant $4$ does not affect the limit at infinity. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{13}=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }(4{x}^{13})=-\\infty .\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-336891/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a5bea806-2913-57f5-ba32-be7aeda5bd15"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-336891"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-300762/field-1/b67476ffce451bf9cc94e82f22f7f4f4b23075d2e02b9ea63c2ad56fd611692c" :answer/id #uuid "cd39d08a-28d3-552c-a980-5df424f6a7f4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-300762/field-1/e7bc415b91e8e2558c763c56ce6657ae30e51a7e3c0c90c1d4cd29cdacdf8934" :answer/id #uuid "c15176a3-1b23-596e-b17c-02491bed22ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/ensure [:answer/v